WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.401 --> 00:00:01.780
- Good morning believe nation!

00:00:01.780 --> 00:00:04.202
My name is Evan Carmichael,
my one word is believe!

00:00:04.202 --> 00:00:05.553
And I believe that entrepreneurs

00:00:05.553 --> 00:00:08.119
will solve all of the
world's major problems.

00:00:08.119 --> 00:00:09.163
So, to help you on your journey,

00:00:09.163 --> 00:00:12.928
today's message is keep
things in perspective.

00:00:12.928 --> 00:00:15.105
Over to you Dalai Lama.

00:00:15.105 --> 00:00:17.126
(rooster crowing)

00:00:17.126 --> 00:00:19.656
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:19.656 --> 00:00:22.361
♫ Espresso keep me going

00:00:22.361 --> 00:00:26.488
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:26.488 --> 00:00:28.500
- [Woman] Do you believe
that your time on here,

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.502
on earth, has been a success?

00:00:30.502 --> 00:00:31.335
- Success?

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:36.100
That all so relative.

00:00:37.665 --> 00:00:41.183
Some feel it, in some which you can tell,

00:00:41.183 --> 00:00:44.766
and some feel failure,
so difficult to say.

00:00:46.268 --> 00:00:49.260
I hope all human life like that,

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:52.230
some part failure, some part achieve.

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:54.313
(laughs)

00:00:55.205 --> 00:00:58.038
It is wrong, to pick up one thing,

00:00:59.055 --> 00:01:01.972
then say all rest success is wrong.

00:01:02.818 --> 00:01:07.200
And pick up one thing, of
failure, that also wrong.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.573
Things are relative.

00:01:08.573 --> 00:01:09.734
- I think one of the hardest things

00:01:09.734 --> 00:01:11.798
that people have a hard
time grasping about me,

00:01:11.798 --> 00:01:13.151
and the people on my team,

00:01:13.151 --> 00:01:16.665
is the ability to pull from
both ends that seem opposite,

00:01:16.665 --> 00:01:18.871
but really, I find, come together.

00:01:18.871 --> 00:01:23.086
To be very humble, and
feel like I know nothing,

00:01:23.086 --> 00:01:26.522
and also feel very confident,
and feel like I know a lot,

00:01:26.522 --> 00:01:29.088
Right, just depending on
what the circumstances are.

00:01:29.088 --> 00:01:30.722
And so, as an example,
look at my youtube channel.

00:01:30.722 --> 00:01:32.890
I'm always struggling to
get better and feel like

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:34.251
I have a lot of work to do.

00:01:34.251 --> 00:01:36.492
And I feel like, I have
a really good channel,

00:01:36.492 --> 00:01:38.811
and I'm super proud of
everything that we've done.

00:01:38.811 --> 00:01:40.254
Both, at the same time.

00:01:40.254 --> 00:01:43.493
I hold both concepts in
my head at the same time.

00:01:43.493 --> 00:01:46.105
And so, depending on who I'm talking to,

00:01:46.105 --> 00:01:47.638
I might be really hard on myself.

00:01:47.638 --> 00:01:50.388
I might be saying, my
channel, I need to get better.

00:01:50.388 --> 00:01:53.489
I need to create better content.

00:01:53.489 --> 00:01:54.760
I need to research to be better.

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:56.338
I need to be better in
front of the camera.

00:01:56.338 --> 00:01:57.597
I need my editing to be better.

00:01:57.597 --> 00:01:59.500
I need everything about
my channel to be better.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:01.686
Because yeah, we've hit a quarter million,

00:02:01.686 --> 00:02:02.913
or three quarters of million subscribers,

00:02:02.913 --> 00:02:05.470
but I know I can do so much more.

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:07.340
I need to get really, really, really good.

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:08.976
I look at who else is out there.

00:02:08.976 --> 00:02:10.837
I look at Les Brown, and Tony Robbins

00:02:10.837 --> 00:02:13.722
and I see their ability to
connect and reach people,

00:02:13.722 --> 00:02:14.761
and I want to do that.

00:02:14.761 --> 00:02:16.468
I need to hone my skills and get better.

00:02:16.468 --> 00:02:18.700
Just drive and improve.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:20.523
And a lot of times people
look at me and say,

00:02:20.523 --> 00:02:22.769
like, that's okay, this is crazy.

00:02:22.769 --> 00:02:25.662
You should be proud of yourself,

00:02:25.662 --> 00:02:26.670
like, look at what you've done.

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:28.647
You've got 100,000,000 views
on your youtube channel.

00:02:28.647 --> 00:02:31.577
You've got 750,000 plus subscribers.

00:02:31.577 --> 00:02:32.855
You're making three videos a day

00:02:32.855 --> 00:02:34.728
where most people can't
even do one a week.

00:02:34.728 --> 00:02:36.922
Like, why are you so hard on yourself?

00:02:36.922 --> 00:02:38.634
Your channel's really good!

00:02:38.634 --> 00:02:39.656
And then my default thinking is

00:02:39.656 --> 00:02:42.849
well, I know my channel's amazing.

00:02:42.849 --> 00:02:44.505
My channel's insanely good.

00:02:44.505 --> 00:02:46.619
I think my channel's the
best channel in the world.

00:02:46.619 --> 00:02:49.687
I think I might be the first
YouTuber to win a Nobel Prize

00:02:49.687 --> 00:02:50.574
for entrepreneurship.

00:02:50.574 --> 00:02:52.174
Like, it's awesome.

00:02:52.174 --> 00:02:54.984
The work I'm doing is meaningful
and having a huge impact.

00:02:54.984 --> 00:02:56.589
It's just pulling from both spectrums,

00:02:56.589 --> 00:03:01.098
like I am so proud of everything
that me and my team make,

00:03:01.098 --> 00:03:03.052
and so filled with confidence
in what we're doing,

00:03:03.052 --> 00:03:05.423
and I know that the impact is real.

00:03:05.423 --> 00:03:07.256
And, what we do sucks.

00:03:09.312 --> 00:03:10.267
It isn't good enough.

00:03:10.267 --> 00:03:13.390
It needs to get better, consistently.

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:16.731
I think that perspective helps.

00:03:16.731 --> 00:03:18.987
I think you have to be able
to pull from both sides.

00:03:18.987 --> 00:03:20.664
I think it applies to a
lot of things in life.

00:03:20.664 --> 00:03:21.872
I look at leadership.

00:03:21.872 --> 00:03:24.022
I look at the people under me on my team.

00:03:24.022 --> 00:03:27.830
I look at sometimes I
need to push them harder.

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:30.202
Sometimes, they might
be like they're amazing

00:03:30.202 --> 00:03:32.054
and you say, well, you know
what, you kind of suck.

00:03:32.054 --> 00:03:34.046
Like, let's go, I know
you can do way better.

00:03:34.046 --> 00:03:35.789
Sometimes, I need to
build them up and say,

00:03:35.789 --> 00:03:38.382
listen, you're amazing, but why
are you so hard on yourself,

00:03:38.382 --> 00:03:40.143
depending on what they need in the moment.

00:03:40.143 --> 00:03:41.455
You know, Alex, who comes in

00:03:41.455 --> 00:03:43.388
who's a part of Toronto Dance Salsa.

00:03:43.388 --> 00:03:47.870
If he comes in and he's
really low and feeling down,

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:49.837
I need to build him back up and say,

00:03:49.837 --> 00:03:54.031
you are really good, like,
you're a really good instructor.

00:03:54.031 --> 00:03:56.078
You don't need to be that hard on yourself

00:03:56.078 --> 00:03:58.093
because the negative energy prevents

00:03:58.093 --> 00:04:00.493
him from doing amazing things.

00:04:00.493 --> 00:04:01.909
Sometimes he comes in so hot, he's like,

00:04:01.909 --> 00:04:03.647
I'm the best thing
ever, and you know what,

00:04:03.647 --> 00:04:04.553
you suck, dude.

00:04:04.553 --> 00:04:05.386
Like, here are all the things

00:04:05.386 --> 00:04:07.556
that you need to off and get better at,

00:04:07.556 --> 00:04:09.071
because the positive energy, again,

00:04:09.071 --> 00:04:12.929
prevents him from going
off and doing great things.

00:04:12.929 --> 00:04:14.781
When you can hold both,

00:04:14.781 --> 00:04:16.307
when you can stay balanced,

00:04:16.307 --> 00:04:18.918
when you can either be in the
middle or pull from both ends

00:04:18.918 --> 00:04:22.290
at the same time, I think
that's where you not just have

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:26.905
more success, but you also
feel more grounded, more happy,

00:04:26.905 --> 00:04:31.072
more secure, less concerned
about other people's thoughts,

00:04:32.431 --> 00:04:35.179
less insecure about
yourself, and your ideas,

00:04:35.179 --> 00:04:36.682
and where you're going.

00:04:36.682 --> 00:04:40.035
So, finding ways constantly
to both be grateful

00:04:40.035 --> 00:04:41.163
for everything you have.

00:04:41.163 --> 00:04:44.093
I'm so grateful for everything in my life.

00:04:44.093 --> 00:04:46.105
I think I have such an amazing life.

00:04:46.105 --> 00:04:48.177
I don't want anybody
else's life out there.

00:04:48.177 --> 00:04:49.152
I want my life.

00:04:49.152 --> 00:04:51.296
I love this life, it's great.

00:04:51.296 --> 00:04:53.046
And, I want to do more.

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:55.690
I want to help more.

00:04:55.690 --> 00:04:57.057
I need to push harder.

00:04:57.057 --> 00:04:58.584
So, it's both, constantly.

00:04:58.584 --> 00:05:00.940
I think if you are only pushing,

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:02.836
only pushing, pushing, pushing, pushing,

00:05:02.836 --> 00:05:04.624
then you're never happy.

00:05:04.624 --> 00:05:05.744
You're never happy, and there's always

00:05:05.744 --> 00:05:08.229
a place to push, right?

00:05:08.229 --> 00:05:10.495
There's always something else you can do

00:05:10.495 --> 00:05:12.790
when you're never happy
with what you've done.

00:05:12.790 --> 00:05:15.057
And, if you're only happy,
and you're only grateful,

00:05:15.057 --> 00:05:17.132
and you're only playing down here,

00:05:17.132 --> 00:05:19.235
then you feel great and you feel blessed,

00:05:19.235 --> 00:05:22.577
which is awesome, but then
you're not contributing enough.

00:05:22.577 --> 00:05:25.081
Like, I feel if you have
the ability to help,

00:05:25.081 --> 00:05:27.552
then you have a responsibility
to help as well.

00:05:27.552 --> 00:05:31.281
If you have all this great
natural talent and ability,

00:05:31.281 --> 00:05:33.981
and you're able to work hard to be able to

00:05:33.981 --> 00:05:35.628
improve other people's
lives but you're not

00:05:35.628 --> 00:05:38.225
because you're just
happy relaxed, laid back,

00:05:38.225 --> 00:05:40.785
I don't think that's a
fulfilling life either.

00:05:40.785 --> 00:05:41.975
I think you need both.

00:05:41.975 --> 00:05:45.183
You need the gratitude
and you need the push,

00:05:45.183 --> 00:05:46.395
that I'm not good enough.

00:05:46.395 --> 00:05:48.187
I have everything I
need and I have nothing,

00:05:48.187 --> 00:05:50.104
both, same time, right.

00:05:51.081 --> 00:05:52.840
And so, keeping things in perspective,

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:55.037
I think, maybe, when people meet me

00:05:55.037 --> 00:05:58.029
it's one of the hardest things
to wrap their heads around.

00:05:58.029 --> 00:06:00.264
I'm having a discussion
still with Alex around today,

00:06:00.264 --> 00:06:02.782
and he's still confused by
some of the things I'm saying,

00:06:02.782 --> 00:06:05.744
and we've been working
together for a while now,

00:06:05.744 --> 00:06:07.067
but I think it's super important.

00:06:07.067 --> 00:06:10.420
And, if you can find that
perspective and keep that balance,

00:06:10.420 --> 00:06:13.505
it'll help you be that
best version of yourself.

00:06:13.505 --> 00:06:16.715
The question day-to-day is, I'm curious,

00:06:16.715 --> 00:06:20.708
what are two opposite ideas
that you hold at the same time

00:06:20.708 --> 00:06:22.102
that the outside world may look at you

00:06:22.102 --> 00:06:23.193
and think you're crazy,

00:06:23.193 --> 00:06:25.370
Like, how can you believe this, and this,

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:27.419
but you see them as just
opposite sides of the coin,

00:06:27.419 --> 00:06:29.221
and you can hold both at the same time.

00:06:29.221 --> 00:06:30.641
What are those two thoughts for you?

00:06:30.641 --> 00:06:33.050
I'm really curious to find
out and I hope you'll share it

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:34.243
down in the comments below.

00:06:34.243 --> 00:06:36.321
I also want to give a quick
shoutout to Erik Peterson.

00:06:36.321 --> 00:06:38.043
Erik, thank you so much for picking up

00:06:38.043 --> 00:06:40.199
a copy of my book, Your One Word.

00:06:40.199 --> 00:06:42.016
I really appreciate the support, man,

00:06:42.016 --> 00:06:43.824
and I hope you enjoy the read.

00:06:43.824 --> 00:06:45.218
So, thank you guys so much for watching.

00:06:45.218 --> 00:06:46.329
I believe in you.

00:06:46.329 --> 00:06:48.190
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:06:48.190 --> 00:06:50.395
and whatever your one word is.

00:06:50.395 --> 00:06:52.738
Much love, and I'll see
you again tomorrow morning

00:06:52.738 --> 00:06:55.155
for another shot of Espresso.

00:06:56.253 --> 00:06:58.592
♫ I wake up every morning

00:06:58.592 --> 00:07:02.009
♫ Espresso keep me going

00:07:05.178 --> 00:07:06.792
- When you have a down time,

00:07:06.792 --> 00:07:09.871
like your something didn't
work the way you want,

00:07:09.871 --> 00:07:12.265
what do you do to get
back on that positiveness?

00:07:12.265 --> 00:07:14.096
- I talk to the mountain.

00:07:14.096 --> 00:07:15.942
I have a mountain next to my home

00:07:15.942 --> 00:07:17.604
that is called wa-she-de-lay.

00:07:17.604 --> 00:07:19.983
It goes up to two, it's very low.

00:07:19.983 --> 00:07:24.055
It goes up to 2,000 and
four, or 2,500 meters.

00:07:24.055 --> 00:07:27.262
Or I talk to the dom-de-me-de,
which is 4,000 meters,

00:07:27.262 --> 00:07:29.810
which is a little bit
further away from my home,

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:31.926
which I see every morning.

00:07:31.926 --> 00:07:34.183
I see those two mountains.

00:07:34.183 --> 00:07:35.905
And I say to these two mountains,

00:07:35.905 --> 00:07:38.701
mountain, I have a problem.

00:07:38.701 --> 00:07:42.343
But, when I look at you,
you are 37 million old.

00:07:42.343 --> 00:07:44.143
(laughing)

00:07:44.143 --> 00:07:45.667
- I'm a little guy,
- you seem to be gay

00:07:45.667 --> 00:07:47.030
and strong.
- I'm a little guy

00:07:47.030 --> 00:07:50.525
here with little
problems, can you help me?

00:07:50.525 --> 00:07:53.233
They would say, come on.

00:07:53.233 --> 00:07:57.961
Look, what we have already
seen in so many years,

00:07:57.961 --> 00:08:00.794
your problem is a none-no problem.

00:08:02.088 --> 00:08:02.932
Come on, get up.

00:08:02.932 --> 00:08:05.731
- Just get up, do it, that's it.

00:08:05.731 --> 00:08:09.898
- 13 years ago you were
completely bust, gone, no money.

00:08:11.306 --> 00:08:13.129
You had five pounds, you know,

00:08:13.129 --> 00:08:15.083
seven dollars left in your hand.

00:08:15.083 --> 00:08:17.785
You used it for a taxi
back to your parents.

00:08:17.785 --> 00:08:19.386
And your career in the record business

00:08:19.386 --> 00:08:22.878
had just come to a sudden shuddering halt.

00:08:22.878 --> 00:08:24.727
Here you are, 13 years later, you're worth

00:08:24.727 --> 00:08:26.253
hundreds of millions of dollars.

00:08:26.253 --> 00:08:28.386
Life's taken a huge turn upwards,

00:08:28.386 --> 00:08:31.086
but I get the sense with you
that what happened to you

00:08:31.086 --> 00:08:33.483
13 years ago still defines you now.

00:08:33.483 --> 00:08:35.495
- Yeah, I mean, it
wasn't the best of times,

00:08:35.495 --> 00:08:37.132
but, then again, when I look back on it,

00:08:37.132 --> 00:08:38.624
it wasn't the worst of times, you know.

00:08:38.624 --> 00:08:42.313
Because, I think, particularly
here in Hollywood,

00:08:42.313 --> 00:08:44.675
where most people get it wrong

00:08:44.675 --> 00:08:47.044
is they're afraid of
losing what they've got.

00:08:47.044 --> 00:08:49.241
They're just terrified
of losing their fame,

00:08:49.241 --> 00:08:51.670
as you said, power, money.

00:08:51.670 --> 00:08:54.223
But actually, once you've lost it,

00:08:54.223 --> 00:08:55.638
you can always get it back again.

00:08:55.638 --> 00:08:56.703
I mean, it's not the end of the world.

00:08:56.703 --> 00:08:58.726
I mean, being ill's worse.

00:08:58.726 --> 00:09:00.961
So, because I've actually faced

00:09:00.961 --> 00:09:03.076
what are most people's worst fears,

00:09:03.076 --> 00:09:06.177
and recovered from it, it's
not as bad as you think.

00:09:06.177 --> 00:09:08.589
You know, I didn't lose my confidence.

00:09:08.589 --> 00:09:10.499
My friends were still my friends.

00:09:10.499 --> 00:09:12.199
I've lived with my parents.

00:09:12.199 --> 00:09:14.079
I just saw it as a challenge

00:09:14.079 --> 00:09:16.314
that I've got to get myself
back on my feet again.

00:09:16.314 --> 00:09:18.716
- What advice do your
parents give you then?

00:09:18.716 --> 00:09:20.023
- They were really, honest to god,

00:09:20.023 --> 00:09:21.490
they were just cool about it.

00:09:21.490 --> 00:09:22.327
They were parents.

00:09:22.327 --> 00:09:24.013
They just wanted to look after me.

00:09:24.013 --> 00:09:25.621
Lived with them for a while.

00:09:25.621 --> 00:09:27.871
My dad lent me some money to pay the bank,

00:09:27.871 --> 00:09:30.665
then eventually I managed
to pay my dad back.

00:09:30.665 --> 00:09:33.351
You know, it was what any parent would do.

00:09:33.351 --> 00:09:38.006
But, like I said, it wasn't
the worst time in my life,

00:09:38.006 --> 00:09:39.472
and actually when I look back on it now,

00:09:39.472 --> 00:09:41.253
I still smile because, you know,

00:09:41.253 --> 00:09:44.589
I bought this beat up
old British sports car,

00:09:44.589 --> 00:09:47.828
worked 18 hours a day, and
I was so determined, Piers,

00:09:47.828 --> 00:09:49.744
just to get it right this time

00:09:49.744 --> 00:09:52.315
'cause I knew why I'd got it wrong.

00:09:52.315 --> 00:09:54.652
Now, I find it much easier
to deal with things.

00:09:54.652 --> 00:09:57.556
- If that wasn't the worst
time in your life, what was?

00:09:57.556 --> 00:09:59.838
- When my dad died was
the worst time in my life.

00:09:59.838 --> 00:10:02.024
As you know, you and I discussed that.

00:10:02.024 --> 00:10:06.006
It was just that, like,
getting ill, losing a parent

00:10:06.006 --> 00:10:07.992
or losing somebody close to you,

00:10:07.992 --> 00:10:10.421
that's the worst thing that
could ever happen in your life.

00:10:10.421 --> 00:10:14.812
And all the things that I
worry about now, just trivial.

00:10:14.812 --> 00:10:17.773
I mean, even the whole kind
of Idol, X Factor thing,

00:10:17.773 --> 00:10:22.352
I laugh about it because it's
such a high-class problem.

00:10:22.352 --> 00:10:24.175
My show's better than your show.

00:10:24.175 --> 00:10:26.343
It's like, but we both got a show.

00:10:26.343 --> 00:10:27.519
(both laughing)

00:10:27.519 --> 00:10:29.442
- It's a bit like Colin
Firth and Hugh Grant

00:10:29.442 --> 00:10:31.147
in the fountain in
Bridget Jones, fighting.

00:10:31.147 --> 00:10:33.504
- Exactly, over what, I mean,

00:10:33.504 --> 00:10:36.606
the fact that we're here, we
can do this, it's fantastic.

00:10:36.606 --> 00:10:38.093
- Keep things in perspective.

00:10:38.093 --> 00:10:39.454
I know we all say it, but really

00:10:39.454 --> 00:10:41.215
you just have to keep reinforcing it.

00:10:41.215 --> 00:10:45.090
Everyone in this audience, knock on wood,

00:10:45.090 --> 00:10:46.727
you're going to do just fine.

00:10:46.727 --> 00:10:49.089
You're going to have
your four-bedroom house.

00:10:49.089 --> 00:10:52.759
You're going to have your
car with power windows.

00:10:52.759 --> 00:10:56.926
You'll be able to go to Sea
World whenever you want to.

00:10:58.021 --> 00:11:00.821
But, along that way, there
are going to be ups and downs.

00:11:00.821 --> 00:11:03.039
And, actually, those ups
and downs tend to happen

00:11:03.039 --> 00:11:04.935
a lot when you go to transition points,

00:11:04.935 --> 00:11:06.612
when you're entering the workforce,

00:11:06.612 --> 00:11:11.326
you're entering some type
of a new phase in your life.

00:11:11.326 --> 00:11:14.446
You just always have to
keep on the long-term game,

00:11:14.446 --> 00:11:15.361
the end game.

00:11:15.361 --> 00:11:17.525
I'll tell you myself, I've gone through

00:11:17.525 --> 00:11:22.498
some fairly dramatic ones
right when I was out the gate.

00:11:22.498 --> 00:11:25.406
This was 1998, I had just graduated,

00:11:25.406 --> 00:11:27.853
I was a few months older
than most of you all,

00:11:27.853 --> 00:11:31.945
and some computer magazine
had seen something I had done,

00:11:31.945 --> 00:11:34.375
and they wrote this neat profile about me.

00:11:34.375 --> 00:11:36.784
They called it Future so Bright.

00:11:36.784 --> 00:11:38.389
I saw that magazine, I
was very proud of it.

00:11:38.389 --> 00:11:40.003
I was like, aw look, I'm set.

00:11:40.003 --> 00:11:42.238
I have this career ahead of me.

00:11:42.238 --> 00:11:43.915
I'm already profiled in a magazine.

00:11:43.915 --> 00:11:47.189
And, frankly, I got kind of in to myself.

00:11:47.189 --> 00:11:50.388
And, that's okay, you
can enjoy your successes,

00:11:50.388 --> 00:11:53.418
but I really didn't have any perspective,

00:11:53.418 --> 00:11:56.956
and, frankly, it was a
dangerous thing to happen.

00:11:56.956 --> 00:11:58.471
To really put things in perspective,

00:11:58.471 --> 00:12:00.887
and I'm happy it did happen,

00:12:00.887 --> 00:12:03.333
two months later I switched jobs,

00:12:03.333 --> 00:12:07.034
higher salary, I thought
I was on the fast track.

00:12:07.034 --> 00:12:11.695
I had a new boss, and on
the first three days of work

00:12:11.695 --> 00:12:14.162
he spent an hour just
completely castigating me.

00:12:14.162 --> 00:12:17.445
Essentially, telling me that
I was not worth his time.

00:12:17.445 --> 00:12:21.793
That I was incompetent as
far as he was concerned.

00:12:21.793 --> 00:12:23.425
You can imagine, you
know, I was like a lot of,

00:12:23.425 --> 00:12:25.655
successful whole life, fancy degrees

00:12:25.655 --> 00:12:27.020
from fancy universities.

00:12:27.020 --> 00:12:29.183
Two months ago I was
profiled in a magazine,

00:12:29.183 --> 00:12:31.579
and then now all of the sudden,

00:12:31.579 --> 00:12:36.159
almost not even 23 years old,
I was back in a hotel room

00:12:36.159 --> 00:12:38.731
in the middle of nowhere crying.

00:12:38.731 --> 00:12:40.540
Not knowing what I was
going to do with my life.

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:42.081
Convinced that it was all
the end of everything,

00:12:42.081 --> 00:12:43.840
that everything was for naught.

00:12:43.840 --> 00:12:45.602
You know, you get through them.

00:12:45.602 --> 00:12:47.703
You wake up in the morning,
things look a little bit better.

00:12:47.703 --> 00:12:49.628
A week later, things look a little better.

00:12:49.628 --> 00:12:50.989
You start circulating your resume around.

00:12:50.989 --> 00:12:54.696
I did find another job two months later.

00:12:54.696 --> 00:12:55.946
And, it's okay.

00:12:57.264 --> 00:12:59.587
The one thing I want to stress,
because I've gone through

00:12:59.587 --> 00:13:01.778
ups and downs, and you
only have one perspective

00:13:01.778 --> 00:13:04.213
for your own life, but I
suspect many of you all

00:13:04.213 --> 00:13:05.818
are going to have higher
ups than I've had,

00:13:05.818 --> 00:13:08.848
and many of you all are
going to have lower downs.

00:13:08.848 --> 00:13:11.265
I worry about that sometimes.

00:13:12.659 --> 00:13:14.900
The ones of you who have higher ups,

00:13:14.900 --> 00:13:16.244
just keep them in perspective.

00:13:16.244 --> 00:13:17.889
It's inevitable, some of
you are going to race ahead

00:13:17.889 --> 00:13:20.531
and be so successful that none of us

00:13:20.531 --> 00:13:22.095
can imagine it right now.

00:13:22.095 --> 00:13:24.960
But, keep them in perspective.

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:27.450
Enjoy the successes, but
when your ego starts feeling

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:29.632
a little bit large, keep in mind

00:13:29.632 --> 00:13:31.856
the sun will supernova one day.

00:13:31.856 --> 00:13:33.940
The galaxies will collide.

00:13:33.940 --> 00:13:36.673
We are these small little
mammals on this small planet,

00:13:36.673 --> 00:13:39.833
or, you know, there's 200
billion stars in the galaxy,

00:13:39.833 --> 00:13:42.404
in just our galaxy alone.

00:13:42.404 --> 00:13:45.565
Just have peace in the little success.

00:13:45.565 --> 00:13:47.913
When you have a hard time, and you will,

00:13:47.913 --> 00:13:49.826
and those who go through painful periods,

00:13:49.826 --> 00:13:51.965
you might stumble and start

00:13:51.965 --> 00:13:53.719
your first few times out the gate,

00:13:53.719 --> 00:13:56.486
also keep in perspective
that those stresses,

00:13:56.486 --> 00:13:58.756
put them in perspective of the universe.

00:13:58.756 --> 00:13:59.812
They are small.

00:13:59.812 --> 00:14:01.021
They're going to be things
that you'll be able to

00:14:01.021 --> 00:14:03.993
laugh about, talk about,
10, 15 years from now,

00:14:03.993 --> 00:14:05.829
or hopefully 10, 15 days from now.

